    "prompt": "Difference between abstract class and interface (Java 8+)?",
    "answerHtml": "<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Abstract class</th>\n<th>Interface</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>State</td>\n<td>Can have instance fields</td>\n<td>Only <code>public static final</code> constants</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Constructor</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Methods</td>\n<td>abstract, concrete, private, static</td>\n<td>abstract, <code>default</code>, <code>static</code>, <code>private</code> (Java 9+)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Inheritance</td>\n<td>Single</td>\n<td>Multiple</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Access modifiers</td>\n<td>any</td>\n<td>methods implicitly <code>public</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Use when</td>\n<td>\"is-a\" with shared state/partial implementation</td>\n<td>capability/contract, multiple inheritance of type</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table><p>Since Java 8, interfaces got <code>default</code> methods so they can carry behaviour — but never <em>state</em>. That's the real remaining distinction. Use an abstract class when subclasses must share fields and a constructor lifecycle; use an interface for capability contracts (<code>Comparable</code>, <code>Serializable</code>, <code>PaymentProcessor</code>).</p>",
