    "part": 10,
    "prompt": "Which design patterns have you used in real projects?",
    "answerHtml": "<p class=\"tldr\"><strong>In one line</strong> — answer with three, each with context — not a list of twenty.</p><ul>\n<li><strong>Strategy</strong> — a <code>List&lt;Handler&gt;</code> injected by Spring, replacing a growing switch on payment or promotion type. Adding a type becomes adding a class.</li>\n<li><strong>Chain of Responsibility</strong> — a validation pipeline. Note that servlet filters and Spring Security's filter chain <em>are</em> this pattern.</li>\n<li><strong>Builder</strong> — immutable request objects with many optional fields, avoiding telescoping constructors.</li>\n<li>If pressed for a fourth: <strong>Template Method</strong> for a family of nightly jobs sharing a skeleton.</li>\n</ul>",
