    "categoryId": "company",
    "companyId": "epam",
    "part": 10,
    "prompt": "Explain Singleton and how you'd implement it safely.",
    "answerHtml": "<p class=\"tldr\"><strong>In one line</strong> — the enum is the safest form; the static holder idiom is the best lazy one.</p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kd\">enum</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Config</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">INSTANCE</span><span class=\"p\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">}</span><span class=\"w\">                    </span><span class=\"c1\">// serialization- and reflection-proof</span>\n</code></pre></div><ul>\n<li>Static holder: lazy <strong>and</strong> thread-safe with no locking.</li>\n<li>Double-checked locking works but <strong>requires <code>volatile</code></strong>, or reordering lets another thread see a partially constructed object.</li>\n</ul><p class=\"bonus\"><strong>Say this too</strong> — in Spring you rarely write any of this — beans are singleton-scoped already. And Singleton is often an anti-pattern: global mutable state, hard to test.</p>",
