    "prompt": "Explain Interface Segregation with a violation and a fix.",
    "answerHtml": "<p class=\"tldr\"><strong>In one line</strong> — no client should be forced to depend on methods it doesn't use.</p><ul>\n<li><strong>Violation:</strong> a fat <code>Worker</code> interface with <code>work()</code> and <code>eat()</code>; <code>Robot</code> implements it by throwing on <code>eat()</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Fix:</strong> split into <code>Workable</code> and <code>Eatable</code>.</li>\n</ul><p class=\"bonus\"><strong>Say this too</strong> — the two violations look identical — a method that throws — because a fat interface <strong>forces</strong> LSP violations. Fixing the interface fixes both. Making that connection is what separates a memorised answer from an understood one.</p>",
